this morning we continued our six-month  investigation into sexual harassment and  assault allegations at the US Air Force  Academy you're seeing it only on CBS  this morning yesterday we shared stories  of more than a dozen current and former  cadets they say they were assaulted at  the school and faced retaliation for  reporting it this morning we aired our  interview with the Academy's former top  official on sexual assault  she told us she believes the Academy  undercounts sexual assault cases over  the past 10 years there have been 287  sexual assault reports made by cadets at  the academy that includes 32 reports  last school year joining us now is the  Air Force Academy's new superintendent  Lieutenant General Jay Silverio general  thank you for being here thank you for  traveling all the way from Colorado  Springs to talk about this we appreciate  it as you know we spoke to cadets who  said they were assaulted they were  harassed even raped and then that they  faced retaliation for reporting it how  do you respond to those cadets  well first off Nora Gail John thank you  for having me it's important that I'm  here to talk about this important topic  and to do this in person face to face  I'm disgusted by those reports I mean  just like you I mean I'm disgusted by  that I'm I'm a parent I've I have two  children that are in their early 20s  that that that went to college there  their mother my wife we'd be horrified  if that happened to one of our children  so I'm just as disgusted by that that  that those assaults happen to those  people just as anyone else well what  about the fact that they tried to report  this to take action they believed they  were retaliated against by their  commanders at the academy so Nora I'm  responsible as a superintendent of the  Academy for the climate and the culture  at the Air Force Academy and I've  committed myself from the very beginning  it's been very clear that I expect the  cadets and the faculty and the staff to  treat each other with respect and  dignity and I've enforced that from the  very beginning but it's also about  accountability and so the office that  was providing the support and I know you  interviewed someone who was in charge of  that office it's about the  accountability of that office and so  there were some allegations that she  about the office but she was in a  leadership role in that office and while  I respect her service in that office  there are standards in that office that  we needed to maintain you ran the piece  about general Johnson that she initiated  the investigation she had to take action  because our cadets deserve the very best  the charissa Beasley says though that  when she brought it to the attention  that it was minimized that the severity  that that that the people were not  believed to nor his point where she said  that they were things were undercut that  people sort of minimalized people at the  academy normalized because it sounds  like she did bring it to the attention  but nothing was done I think that's the  answer we're trying to get from you so  she did she did bring a number of items  to attention which was part of the  investigation and there were a couple  dozen witnesses that were part of the  investigation but I think it's important  that you don't just focus on Teresa  Beasley in that office we have a number  of layers of care that we have for  cadets they have mental health  professionals they have student  counseling services they have chaplains  that are available for cadets of faith I  mean we have all of our commanders are  trained they have an accredited master's  degree in counseling from the University  of Colorado system so cadets get support  the way that they want to get support  not the way that someone in an  individual also thinks they should get  one of the people Norah talked to said  you go there because you want to protect  your country and they don't protect you  that's a pretty extraordinary statement  what you've talked about accountability  what exactly are you doing so that no  cadet would ever be in a position to  have be able to make that kind of a  statement well since I've been there  I've made my stance on the whole journey  I've been there since August since I've  made my stance on on culture and climate  very clear additionally I have held  everyone to a high standard last month  we we removed a cadet that for sexual  assault last month we also removed a  cadet for racial slurs  I've also held out one of our athletic  teams to a very high standard and and  suspended some coaches and players for  not meeting standards so I think what's  important is my role is to set that  climate and set that culture at the  Force Academy as we reported yesterday  two attacks on a trail near the campus  were called physical attacks but were  actually violent rapes why didn't the  Academy warn cadets when that happened  no the Academy did warn cadets the  Academy did an exhaustive investigation  of that trail and of that incident over  80 witnesses were called the  investigation was over months a number  of forensics about when people arrived  and left left the area and then we added  a number of measures and protective  measures onto the trail so the Academy  took that very seriously well in fact  it's my reporting here that the only  public announcement made in the wake of  these attacks was on new sofas Facebook  page and that the the academies  essentially started a buddy system but  they didn't warn that they were raped  they just said they were attacks well  until we could actually confirm what did  what had happened on there through the  through the the investigation and  through the research of the specific  things it was very difficult for general  Johnson at the time to then say what  specifically happened until they could  let me ask you this now do you believe  that two cadets were raped on that trail  I don't know exactly what happened on  the trail but let me make it very very  clear Nora that whatever happened on the  trail the most important thing is that  those cadets get support and that they  get care and that their voices are heard  and they have an opportunity to talk  about some with someone to receive that  case do you think there is a problem  general at the academy when it comes to  sexual assaults and how it's being  handled do you think that there are  problems there I don't think there are  problems and let me tell you why is that  you talked about some of the reporting  of the numbers of sexual assaults I want  numbers of reports to go up I know that  doesn't make sense right on the surface  but I want reporting to go up we know  that this is an underreported crime we  know that across the nation and across  campuses this is underreported so I went  reporting to go up so that I can provide  that care provide that support last year  38 percent of the assaults that were  reported to our offices 30  8% were assaults that happened prior to  arriving at the Air Force Academy which  means someone Etsy and support for  something that happened prior to  arriving at the Academy that's why I  want to have that available to them that  support all right general Salaria thank  you so much for being here for this  conversation we really appreciate it  well thank you very much

